## Who is the audit for?

My £495 technical SEO audit suits a site with a defined technical question or an unexplained organic-search problem. Common triggers include:

- important pages missing from Google
- a traffic drop after a migration or platform change
- duplicate or parameter URLs consuming crawl attention
- templates with inconsistent canonical or indexation rules
- slow or unstable page experiences
- a development backlog that needs evidence-based priorities
- an agency contract coming up for renewal
- a new site or directory that has not launched yet

A brand new site with fewer than 10 pages needs a smaller scope. Weak demand for what the site sells calls for a different service. So does a team that needs someone to implement every change.

## Can the audit check an SEO agency's work?

Yes. The same £495 technical SEO audit fee covers it. I audit the site and compare your agency's reports with what actually changed on the site and in Search Console.

The [independent SEO agency review](/services/seo-agency-review/) page covers the full process, what to send and the questions to ask before you renew.

## Does the audit cover mobile SEO?

Yes. The technical SEO audit covers mobile SEO because Google crawls and ranks the mobile version of a site first. The audit checks that version. That includes responsive behaviour, mobile usability, whether important content is available on mobile and Core Web Vitals field data across device types. A separate mobile-only audit is not needed for a standard site.

## What happens after the audit?

Start with the highest-impact validated actions once the technical SEO audit is delivered. Assign each action to an owner and record the acceptance check before implementation begins.

Measure crawling and indexation separately from rankings and enquiries. A corrected technical issue can remove a constraint without creating demand or making a page more relevant than its competitors.

Implementation remains a separate decision. You can use your existing developer or request a separate implementation scope after reviewing the findings.

